legal issues and a trial,

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:38.810
so I am really looking forward

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.610
to reviewing what happens in this episode.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.080
As always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection,

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.630
which I will either sustain or overrule.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.310
And stick around until
the end of the video,

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.780
where I give the series
finale for Seinfeld

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.070
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:53.950
So, without further ado,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.860
let's dig into the finale of Seinfeld.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.780
(Seinfeld show music)

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.160
Okay, so what has happened
in this episode so far,

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.290
which is not legal per se,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.300
but I think it sets up
the rest of this episode,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.020
the gang decides to go
on a vacation in Paris.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.410
NBC gives them the use
of their private jet.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.500
Because they were screwing
around in the air,

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.830
the pilot had to do an emergency landing

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.280
in this very small town in Massachusetts,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.200
and so they are just walking
around this random town,

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.840
deciding what they're going to do next.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:28.933
And I think this is where the
legal stuff kicks into gear.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:31.553
- Nice day.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.230
- Another one?
(audience laughs)

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.260
- [Carjacker] Alright
fatso, out of the car!

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.420
- I'm gonna capture this.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.460
- Come on!
(driver grunting and yelling)

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.600
- All right, so we've got a carjacking.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.700
- Gimme your wallet!
- Don't shoot!

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.680
- So the robber has
his hand in his pocket,

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.970
like there's a gun.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:50.950
- See the great thing
about robbin' a fat guy

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.860
is it's an easy getaway, you know?

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.940
They can't really chase ya.
(audience laughs)

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:56.780
- He's actually doin'--

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.173
- All right, some kinda
crappy things to say.

00:01:59.173 --> 00:02:00.702
(audience laughs)

00:02:00.702 --> 00:02:02.264
- Get your wallet, come on!

00:02:02.264 --> 00:02:03.323
Come on, come on!

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:05.200
- Oh, that's a shame.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.260
- I guess 'cause they're New
Yorkers they're not gonna help.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.690
- Officer, he's stealing my car!

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.100
Officer, I was carjacked,
I was held up at gunpoint,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.540
and he took my wallet and everything.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.310
- Okay, thanks anyway.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.520
They can't get another plane.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.300
- All right, what's wrong
with they plane we got?

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.133
They're just checking--

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:21.240
- 'Kay, there's videotape evidence.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.790
All right, I think I
know where this is going.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.090
- I'm not getting on there.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.030
Come on, let's get something
to eat here in Sticksville.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.933
- All right, hold it right there.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.260
- Okay, why isn't the
police officer calling in

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.050
for backup and chasing down this guy

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.590
who just committed a carjacking here?

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.920
You'd think that, if
someone was robbed at,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.540
ostensibly, gunpoint, we didn't see a gun,

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.140
but the guy had his hand in his pocket

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.530
and gesturing it towards the
poor guy who is being robbed

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:50.770
and makes it seem like there's a gun.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.130
That is armed robbery.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.120
That is grand larceny in the first degree,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.810
and that's a major felony.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.380
So I don't know why this police officer's

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.300
being so nonchalant about
a first-degree felony

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.160
that has just occurred, and
he's letting the bad guy get

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.340
away here.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.770
- Hold it right there.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.750
- What?

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:09.820
- You're under arrest.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:10.653
- Under arrest?

00:03:10.653 --> 00:03:11.486
What for?
- What?

00:03:11.486 --> 00:03:15.313
- Article 223-7 of the
Latham County penal code.

00:03:15.313 --> 00:03:16.146
- What?

00:03:16.146 --> 00:03:17.325
No, no, we didn't do anything.

00:03:17.325 --> 00:03:19.030
(Kramer scoffs)
- That's exactly right.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.610
The law requires you to help
or assist anyone in danger

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:23.443
- What?
as long

00:03:23.443 --> 00:03:25.000
as it's reasonable to do so.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.350
- I never knew that.
- No, that's not a thing.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.410
- It's called The Good Samaritan Law.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.620
- Okay, this is really
interesting from my perspective.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.160
So the writers of the show
are conflating a couple

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.810
of different things here.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.910
First of all, there is such a
thing as Good Samaritan laws.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:43.130
A Good Samaritan law is an exception

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:47.050
to a general responsibility
when you choose to act.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.740
To back up a little bit
further, in American law,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.300
generally, you do not have an obligation

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.010
to help anyone under any circumstances

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.220
unless you undertake some action

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:00.180
and the law imputes a duty to you.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.990
So the classic example in law school

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.540
is if you see someone drowning in a pool.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:09.170
You are not responsible for
helping them out of the pool.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:12.150
But if you choose to
actually help them get

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.890
out of the pool where they're drowning

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.850
and you do so in a negligent way,

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:18.810
well, then you can be held responsible

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.530
for the damages that you cause

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:21.850
to this poor person that was drowning,

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.920
even though you undertook
to save their life.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.400
And so the general rule is
you do not have any kind

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.260
of obligation to help your fellow man.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.210
And as New Yorkers,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.080
they're clearly exercising
that normal right.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.830
Now a Good Samaritan law is
an exception to that rule

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.750
that says if someone is in danger

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.880
or you undertake to help that person

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.310
where you wouldn't normally
have an obligation,

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.030
you are protected by law so
that they can't turn around

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.150
and sue you if you help them
get out of a drowning pool

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.910
or you perform CPR on
someone that was drowning

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:59.190
or you perform the Heimlich Maneuver.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:03.220
Normally, without a Good
Samaritan law, you might be liable

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.900
for negligently performing
the Heimlich Maneuver

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.560
or negligently performing CPR.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.650
But a Good Samaritan law is a defense

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.710
for good samaritans who help out.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:13.720
Okay, so that's the first thing.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.700
That's what a Good
Samaritan law actually is.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.860
Now, number two is that, generally,

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.830
there are no affirmative
duties like this police officer

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.220
has said, that there is some law that says

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.350
that you have to undertake
to actually help people.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:32.660
Now there are a couple of
exceptions to that rule,

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:33.690
but let me see if they talk

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.340
about this so-called Good
Samaritan law in more detail

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.010
to get an idea of what
they're dealing with here.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.870
- A Good Samaritan law?

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.280
Are they crazy?

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:42.561
- Why would we wanna help somebody?

00:05:42.561 --> 00:05:43.394
- I know.
(chuckles)

00:05:43.394 --> 00:05:45.757
- That's what nuns and
Red Cross workers are for.

00:05:45.757 --> 00:05:46.860
(audience laughs)
- The Samaritans

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.165
were an ancient tribe,
very helpful to people.

00:05:50.165 --> 00:05:51.090
(audience laughs)

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:52.890
- All right. (sighs with exasperation)

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:54.370
Um, excuse me?

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.380
Hi.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.260
Could ya tell me what kind of law this is?

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.360
- Ooh, yeah, let's hear
about the law! (claps hands)

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.690
- Well, they just passed it last year,

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.490
and it's modeled after the French law.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:05.323
- Okay, that's new.
- I heard about it

00:06:05.323 --> 00:06:06.670
after Princess Diana was killed,

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.124
and all those photographers
were just standing around.

00:06:09.124 --> 00:06:10.493
- Oh, yeah.
- Oh, yeah.

00:06:10.493 --> 00:06:11.326
(audience laughs)
- You're the first ones

00:06:11.326 --> 00:06:13.737
to be arrested on it,
probably in the whole country.

00:06:13.737 --> 00:06:14.570
- Oh.
(Elaine laughs)

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:16.790
- Okay, so it's not often
enforced; that makes sense.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:18.100
- Well, let's pay the fine or something

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:18.933
and get the hell outta here.

00:06:18.933 --> 00:06:20.220
- Well, it's not that easy.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.907
See now, the law calls for
a maximum fine of $85,000,

00:06:23.907 --> 00:06:25.030
- What?
- and as much

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.968
as five years in prison.
- What?

00:06:26.968 --> 00:06:27.801
- What?

00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:29.220
- Oh, no, no, no, no.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.450
We have to be in California next week!

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:32.780
We're starting a TV show!

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:33.613
- California?

00:06:33.613 --> 00:06:35.240
Oh gosh, I don't think so.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.324
You know, my guess is
you're gonna be prosecuted.

00:06:37.324 --> 00:06:38.157
(laughs)

00:06:38.157 --> 00:06:39.750
Better get yourselves a good lawyer.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.150
- Okay, all right, let's
unpack this a little bit here.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:44.500
So what they're talking about here

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.880
is not actually a Good Samaritan law.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.680
It's actually what's called
a Duty to Rescue law.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.320
As I said before, normally,
you don't have a duty

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.490
to rescue people.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:57.830
You are free to walk by an injured person

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.157
without having to help,
and a Good Samaritan law

00:07:00.157 --> 00:07:03.110
is the thing that actually
gives you a defense

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.930
if something goes wrong when
you choose to help someone.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:09.690
But a duty to rescue does impute a duty

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:14.690
on you as a passerby
to actually intervene.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:18.940
Now those are very, very rare
because we don't want people

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.950
to have to intervene in all circumstances,

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.910
especially in instances like we saw

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.010
with an armed carjacking.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.930
What were they supposed to do there?

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.120
Presumably, the guy had a gun.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.170
So the Duty to Rescue laws exist,

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:34.050
but they're very, very rare.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.220
I know that California does
actually have a variation

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.240
on a Duty to Rescue law.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.200
It's not a duty to rescue
and certainly nothing

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.720
like what you're seeing in Seinfeld,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:46.380
but it is a duty to
report in the wake of some

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.780
of the reports of child
abuse and child endangerment.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.300
California passed a law that said

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.610
that you had to report
certain crimes being committed

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:57.620
against a child under the age of 14.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.610
But even there, it's not a felony

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:03.130
to fail to report the crime.

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:08.130
It is, I think, a misdemeanor
and a fine of 1,500 or $2,000.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:10.220
There's no way that you're likely going

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.980
to have an American law somewhere

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:14.530
that says it is a major felony,

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:17.410
up to five years in jail,
to fail to intervene

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.150
in another felony being committed.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.477
- Who told you to put the cheese on?

00:08:21.477 --> 00:08:22.940
Did I tell you to put the cheese on?

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:24.109
I didn't tell you to put the cheese on.

00:08:24.109 --> 00:08:24.942
(audience laughs)
(buzzer buzzes)

00:08:24.942 --> 00:08:25.950
- [Receptionist] Jerry
Seinfeld on the phone.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:28.503
- You people with the
cheese; it never ends.

00:08:28.503 --> 00:08:29.730
(audience laughs)

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.050
Hello?

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.170
Mm-hmm.

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:33.830
Mm-hmm.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:34.663
Mm-hmm.

00:08:34.663 --> 00:08:35.496
- So this guy is clearly

00:08:35.496 --> 00:08:36.770
like Johnny Cochran.
- Good Samaritan law?

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.080
I never heard of it.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.500
You don't have to help anybody.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.333
- Yeah.
- That's what this country

00:08:40.333 --> 00:08:41.166
is all about.
(audience laughs)

00:08:41.166 --> 00:08:42.069
- This lawyer is right.

00:08:42.069 --> 00:08:43.649
(audience laughs)
- Unfathomable, improbable.

00:08:43.649 --> 00:08:44.923
Hold on.

00:08:44.923 --> 00:08:47.220
Susie, cancel my
appointment with Dr. Bison,

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.280
and pack a bag for me.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:51.440
I wanna to get to Latham,
Massachusetts right away.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:52.273
- Nice.

00:08:52.273 --> 00:08:53.800
So

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.290
clearly, a lawyer (laughs) who

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:58.890
is in the mold of the
famous Johnny Cochran,

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.980
who was passed away but was well known

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.580
for being OJ Simpson's attorney.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.030
I probably would have had
a very similar reaction

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:09.300
to that phone call, that
that's a ridiculous law

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.500
that doesn't exist anywhere.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.063
In reality, we'd probably be right.

00:09:14.063 --> 00:09:16.420
(laughs) And number two, doesn't matter.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:19.100
If your client's in jail,
you get outta your office

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:23.060
and you go to your client, and
you deal with whatever kind

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.230
of local nonsense is going on.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:29.030
So yeah, I kinda like
that lawyer character.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.790
- So they got Jackie Chiles, huh?

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:32.940
- Mm-hmm, you know what that means.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.960
This whole place is gonna be swarming

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.370
with media by the time this thing is over.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:40.520
You're not gonna be able to
find a hotel room in this town.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:43.240
- Astute Legal Eagles will
recognize that character actor,

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:44.740
whose name escapes me at the moment,

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:48.280
but he was the expert
witness in My Cousin Vinny,

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.220
talking about the tire marks that

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.060
were made in front of the Sack of Suds.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.840
So it's good to see that he got promoted

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.740
from expert witness to
local district attorney.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.180
- The whole country is
going to be watching us.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.250
Now we gotta do whatever--
- (laughs) And I think

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.140
he's using the same accent, too.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.400
- No matter what the cost.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.520
You know, now the big issue in this trial

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.730
is going to be character.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.980
- Mm, that is absolutely
not true. (laughs)

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.430
Character actually is figuratively
the least important thing

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:19.200
in a criminal trial.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.000
And, in fact, the prosecution
doesn't even have the option

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.270
to bring in character evidence
in a situation like this.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:27.510
- Fourth District County
Court, Latham, Massachusetts,

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:28.840
is now in session.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:31.711
Your honorable Judge
Arthur Vandelay presiding.

00:10:31.711 --> 00:10:32.670
(audience laughs)

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.930
- Vandelay?

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.470
The judge's name is Vandelay?

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:36.303
- Vanda-who?

00:10:36.303 --> 00:10:37.823
- Jerry, did you hear that?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:37.823 --> 00:10:39.720
- I think that's a good sign.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.610
- Is the district
attorney ready to proceed?

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:44.300
- We are, Your Honor.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.590
- So are we starting trial here?

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:48.560
They've just totally
skipped the indictment,

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:52.580
the arraignment, all the
pretrial exchange of material,

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:57.580
Brady disclosures,
investigations. (laughs)

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.290
It's a comedy, I know.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.470
No one's gonna wanna watch
all the procedural stuff.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.280
This isn't Law &amp; Order, and, even then,

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:05.120
they will skip through
everything, but man.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.180
This appears to be happening
all in the same day.

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:10.960
So that's a little unrealistic there.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.100
- Ladies and gentlemen, last year,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.670
our city council, by a vote of 12

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:17.540
to two passed a Good Samaritan law.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.960
Now essentially, we made it a crime

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:22.730
to ignore a fellow human being in trouble.

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:24.860
Now this group from
- It's a bad law.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:27.220
- New York not only ignored but,

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:30.940
as we will prove, they
actually mocked the victim

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:32.893
as he was being robbed at gunpoint.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:37.000
I can guarantee you one other
thing, ladies and gentlemen.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.850
This is not the first time they
have behaved in this manner.

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:41.980
- Oh, objection!

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:43.630
Improper character argument.

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.600
- Of mocking and maligning.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.940
This is a history of selfishness,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:52.333
self absorption, immaturity, and greed.

00:11:52.333 --> 00:11:53.270
(audience laughs)
- Uh... (laughs)

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:56.810
- You will see how everyone
who has come into contact

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.760
with these four individuals
has been abused,

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.430
wronged, deceived, and betrayed.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:06.310
- That is completely improper.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:08.710
The American system recognizes that,

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:11.660
just because you have acted
in a certain way in the past

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:13.540
under different circumstances,

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.530
that doesn't mean that you
are going to act in conformity

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:19.440
with that character on a given instance.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:23.240
Generally speaking, criminal
defendants never have

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:26.180
to face the burden of overcoming
the prosecution entering

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:28.290
in bad character evidence
because the prosecution

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:31.460
is never allowed to bring
in character evidence

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:34.360
against the accused because
it's just not simply relevant.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:38.790
As regular people recognize
that it might be relevant

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.680
in an abstract sense, but
we have decided as a society

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:45.280
that it is not relevant for the
purposes of a court hearing.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.110
Now there is an exception
in that the defendant

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:51.600
has the option to bring in
evidence of good character.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.410
If you're simply saying that
they are such a good person

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:57.970
that they couldn't
possibly have committed it,

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:02.130
well, that option resides
with the criminal defendant.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.370
And when you do that,
you open the floodgates,

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:06.760
and then the prosecution is allowed

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:08.690
to rebut evidence of good character

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:10.380
with evidence of bad character.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.540
But you're not allowed to guild the lily

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:16.560
and say not only did they
commit this specific act

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:19.420
or, in this case, an omission,
but they're also bad people.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:20.950
You're not allowed to do that.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:24.430
So the rest of it is
just complete nonsense.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.310
- I am shocked and chagrined!

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:28.440
(audience laughs)
(laughs)

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.670
- It's very much like
Johnny Cochran; I love it.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:32.780
- This trial is outrageous!

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:36.040
It's a waste of the
taxpayers' time and money.

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:38.850
It is a travesty of justice
that these four people

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.460
have been incarcerated
while the real perpetrator

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:45.700
is walking around laughing,
lying and laughing,

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.240
laughing and lying.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:49.073
- I love it.

00:13:49.073 --> 00:13:52.190
You might remember that Johnny
Cochran, the real attorney,

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:55.170
was famous for these alliterative phrases

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:56.890
that he would use in court.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:01.570
And often, people derided
that as being childish.

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:04.540
But it's a little brilliance in trial

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:07.240
because there are a million
things that are going on,

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:10.770
and it is so important to allow the jury

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:12.830
to hang their hat on something

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.020
and to be able to get through to the jury

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.660
and give them something simple,

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:21.330
a theme or a phrase that you
can hammer home at every turn.

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:22.840
It's definitely good trial practice.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:24.580
So I like what he's doing.

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:26.290
He's shocked and chagrined.

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:28.100
- You know what these four people were?

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.480
They were innocent bystanders.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:31.313
- Yeah.
- Now you think

00:14:31.313 --> 00:14:32.146
about that term.

00:14:32.146 --> 00:14:34.090
- Innocent bystanders.
- Innocent

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:36.090
bystanders,
(audience laughs)

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.560
because that's exactly what they were.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:39.764
We know they were bystanders;
nobody's disputing that.

00:14:39.764 --> 00:14:40.597
(laughs)
So how

00:14:40.597 --> 00:14:41.540
can a bystander be guilty?

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:42.580
No such thing!

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:45.500
Have you ever heard of a guilty bystander?

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:49.530
No, because you cannot be
a bystander and be guilty.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:51.410
Bystanders are, by definition, innocent.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:53.560
That is the nature of bystanding!

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.760
- And that is a great
summary of the status quo

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:58.980
of American law, absolutely right.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:01.990
Innocent bystanders are,
by definition, innocent.

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:04.310
In the absence of this
crazy Massachusetts law

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:07.090
that doesn't actually
exist, that's 100% accurate.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:11.010
- Is the prosecution ready
to present its first witness?

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:12.470
- We are, Your Honor.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:15.720
Call Officer Matt Vogel to the stand.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:17.593
- All right.
- Call Matt Vogel.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:24.080
- So the police officer is
not a character witness.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.720
This guy would absolutely
be allowed to testify

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:29.200
because he was a percipient
witness to what happened.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.900
- Did one of them have a video camera?

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:33.250
- Yes.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:34.420
- Your Honor, with the court's permission,

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.460
we would like to play back that video

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:39.490
and enter it into evidence as Exhibit A.

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:41.000
- Proceed.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:42.690
- They haven't established
the proper chain

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.250
of custody there.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.680
Generally, records that
are created outside

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:49.060
of court qualify as hearsay.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:51.550
Regardless of the fact
that it is a recording,

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:54.770
it's still considered a statement

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:56.590
for the purposes of the hearsay rule.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:16:00.380
But given that it was
taken by the defendants,

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:03.500
arguably, that is an admission by a party

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:06.380
or a statement of a party,
so it's probably proper

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:09.380
for that to come in, assuming
proper chain of custody

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:11.930
is made, which the police
officer should establish.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.200
- Come on!
- Okay, I'm getting out,

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:15.033
I'm getting out.
- Well,

00:16:15.033 --> 00:16:17.180
there goes the money
for the lipo. (snickers)

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:19.210
- [Elaine] See, the great
thing about robbin' a fat guy

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:20.720
is it's an easy getaway.

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:23.450
They can't really chase ya.
(Jerry and Elaine laugh)

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:24.870
- But just because you're a bad person

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.500
doesn't mean you committed a crime.

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:28.710
And arguably, if I was
the defense attorney here,

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:31.670
I might stipulate to the fact
that it's unfairly prejudicial

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.470
for the audio to be played
because it's not relevant

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:36.170
to their actions.

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:39.100
You can see from the tape that
they did not help this person

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.020
who was being carjacked,
but the things that they

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:44.510
are saying are so unfairly prejudicial

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.700
that it outweighs the probative value

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:50.047
of the statements that
are actually being made.

00:16:50.047 --> 00:16:52.278
And it's unfair character
evidence to come in.

00:16:52.278 --> 00:16:53.960
So I think that the
attorneys here really should

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.960
have made an objection and fought over

00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:59.220
at least the audio portion of that tape.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:01.650
- Call Mabel Choate to the stand.

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:03.700
- Call Mabel Choate!

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:04.533
- Who?

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:08.250
Who is this person?
- Your Honor,

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.710
I most strenuously and vigorously
object to this witness.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.710
She was not present at
the time of the incident,

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:13.740
her testimony is irrelevant,

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:15.163
irrational, and inconsequential.

00:17:15.163 --> 00:17:16.800
(audience laughs)
- Yep, 100%.

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:17.633
Great objection.
- The prosecution

00:17:17.633 --> 00:17:19.840
has gone to great lengths
and considerable cost

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:21.870
to find these character witnesses.

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:23.740
It is imperative that we establish this

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.990
is not merely an isolated incident.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:28.280
It's part of a pattern
of any social behavior

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.140
that's been going on for years.

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:31.570
- No.
- Objection overruled.

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:33.220
- Pfft.
- I'll hear the witness.

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:37.430
- Yeah, so just because
the prosecution goes

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:39.760
to great expense to find a witness,

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:42.360
that has nothing to do
with whether that witness

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:44.750
can actually testify at court.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:46.670
The judge here is supposed to act

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:49.780
like a gatekeeper and keep out unfair,

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:51.610
extraneous evidence like this.

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:54.130
- Now, Mrs. Choate, would
you please tell the court

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:56.440
what happened the evening of January 4th?

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:58.180
- I was at Schnitzer's Bakery

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:00.537
when I got accosted by that man.

00:18:00.537 --> 00:18:01.740
- [District Attorney] What did he want?

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.101
- My marble rye.

00:18:03.101 --> 00:18:04.123
Someone help!

00:18:04.123 --> 00:18:05.500
- Shut up, you old hag!

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:06.450
- Not admissible.

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:09.150
Funny vehicle for the
finale, but totally improper

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:10.750
from a trial perspective.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.520
- I dated Mr. Seinfeld for several weeks

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.323
in the autumn of 1992.

00:18:15.323 --> 00:18:16.930
- Then the evening of October 28th,

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:19.080
there was an abrupt end
to that relationship.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:20.490
Tell us what happened.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.480
- The four of them made a wager to see

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:27.258
who could go the longest
without gratifying themselves!

00:18:27.258 --> 00:18:30.030
(audience laughs)
(laughs)

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.890
- Okay, improper character witness.

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.830
- [Donald] I challenged him
to a game of Trivial Pursuit.

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:37.368
- It's Moops.
- The Moors!

00:18:37.368 --> 00:18:39.090
(audience laughs)
- Help, someone!

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:40.610
- Also improper character witness.

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:44.020
- Mr. Kramer gave you a used wheelchair?

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:45.358
- That's right.
(audience laughs)

00:18:45.358 --> 00:18:47.450
(wheelchair screeching)
(woman screams)

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:48.283
- Not relevant.

00:18:48.283 --> 00:18:53.270
- So she pretended to trip,
and she fell into your breasts.

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:55.400
- Yes.
(courtroom murmuring)

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.450
He sent her in there to
find out if they were real.

00:18:58.450 --> 00:18:59.810
- Not admissible.

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:01.550
- He was a communist.
- No.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:03.123
- She exposed her nipple.
- Hm-mm.

00:19:03.123 --> 00:19:06.310
- Seinfeld told me to change
the menu to Pakistani,

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:08.196
(audience laughs)
but nobody came.

00:19:08.196 --> 00:19:10.110
- Ugh, improper character.

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:11.950
- Cock fighting?

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:12.900
- Cock fighting?

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.020
- Improper character.

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:16.140
- She tried to smother me with a pillow.

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:17.210
- Improper character.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:20.541
- Well, I invited him to
attend my son's birthday party.

00:19:20.541 --> 00:19:21.532
- Fire!

00:19:21.532 --> 00:19:23.000
(audience laughs)
(kids screaming)

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:24.090
- Improper character.

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:25.368
Oh, charlie horse.

00:19:25.368 --> 00:19:27.550
(laughing in pain) Jesus.

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:29.820
- This courtroom and everyone
who's attended this trial

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:32.040
is still reeling from the endless parade

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:35.010
of witnesses who've come
forth so enthusiastically

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:38.260
to testify against these four
seemingly ordinary people.

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:40.210
One even had the feeling
that if Judge Vandelay

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:41.860
didn't finally put a stop to it,

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:43.530
it could've gone on for months.

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:44.363
- So this is

00:19:44.363 --> 00:19:47.810
why you don't allow irrelevant
character witnesses,

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:49.520
regardless of the expense
of the prosecution

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.770
in finding all of these people.

00:19:51.770 --> 00:19:52.770
It never ends.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.280
It's a parade of people who
really can't answer the question

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:59.120
of whether the defendants
committed the act

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:01.960
that they are accused of
in this specific instance.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:03.440
They might be horrible people,

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:06.560
but it has no relevance to
their criminal prosecution.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:07.890
So all this is improper.

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:09.630
Whew, moving on.

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:12.713
(light, tense music)

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:20.810
So does the defense not get

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:24.150
to make a closing
statement here? (chuckles)

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:26.890
We're just going straight
to jury deliberation.

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:29.203
- Will the defendants please rise?

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:35.240
And how do you find with respect

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.550
to the charge of criminal indifference?

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:39.860
- We find the defendants

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:42.153
guilty.

00:20:42.153 --> 00:20:43.580
(chuckles)
(courtroom gasps)

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:48.090
- Well, as the crime was laid
out, they were guilty of it.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.130
It's just a law that is stupid

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.620
and should not be on the books.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:57.620
- I do not know how or under
what circumstances the four

00:20:57.950 --> 00:21:02.950
of you found each other, but
your careless indifference

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:05.840
and utter disregard for everything

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:10.840
that is good and decent has
rocked the very foundation

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:13.817
upon which our society is built.

00:21:13.817 --> 00:21:14.900
(audience laughs)

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:17.410
I can think of nothing more fitting

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:22.710
than for the four of you
to spend a year removed

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:27.450
from society so that you can contemplate

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:31.940
the manner in which you
have conducted yourselves.

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:33.200
- So in real life,

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:36.090
there would be a separate
sentencing hearing.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:39.060
In most places, there are
guidelines that you have to follow

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:42.640
to make sure that there aren't
manifestly unjust sentences

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:43.770
that are brought down.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.850
You wouldn't have the sentencing hearing

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:51.750
immediately following the actual
guilt portion of the trial

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:53.810
because there are all
kinds of other factors

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:57.040
that go into how much
of a sentence they get.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.100
The judge here has decided a year

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:03.740
on a maximum of five years,
whereas you're gonna have

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:06.080
to take into account a
lot of different kinds

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:07.560
of evidence than what was elicited

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:10.250
at the actual initial trial.

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:13.840
So that would take place
weeks or months later.

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.950
There would be probably
an immediate appeal

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:19.490
given the ridiculous number
of character witnesses

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:22.340
that were called in,
which was clearly unjust

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:24.380
and unduly prejudicial.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.710
And yeah, this conviction is totally

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:28.642
gonna get overturned on appeal.

00:22:28.642 --> 00:22:32.490
(bright, upbeat, plucky music)

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:36.200
Okay, that was the series
finale of Seinfeld.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.033
Funnier than I remember.

00:22:37.033 --> 00:22:38.130
I might have to actually go back

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:39.630
and watch a few more episodes of Seinfeld

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:41.880
because maybe I just
couldn't appreciate it

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:43.420
when I was a younger man.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:45.620
But now it's time to give Seinfeld a grade

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:49.930
for legal realism.
(gavel bangs)

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:53.080
On one hand, you have a discussion of laws

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:55.670
that kind of exist in some circumstances.

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:58.240
There's no such thing
as criminal indifference

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:02.160
that I'm aware of in any US
jurisdiction, maybe in France.

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:04.980
But there are Duty to Report laws,

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:07.740
and so there are sometimes duties

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:09.410
that are imputed to individuals

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.510
who would otherwise be
an innocent bystander.

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:14.230
And I really liked the
character of Jackie Chiles.

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:17.410
He is a simulacrum of Johnny Cochran,

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:21.430
same initials, same litigation style.

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:24.310
It's a little cartoonish,
but also indicative

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:27.050
of some really good trial
practice in there as well.

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:31.730
On the other hand, you have
dozens of improper witnesses.

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:35.070
You have a law that would
not exist in real life.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:36.340
So the whole premise for the show

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:39.000
is thrown into question,
and you have a timeline

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:44.000
where they are convicted of
a major felony in the span of

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:45.990
48 hours, maybe?

00:23:45.990 --> 00:23:47.790
So all in all, I'm going to have

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:52.620
to give the finale of
Seinfeld a D plus for realism.

